# Ultra AutoTrade — Cursor Rules

## Project Context
You are developing Ultra AutoTrade: a cryptocurrency automated trading system combining
OctoBot, AI (Claude + GPT-4o), Aave V3, and Bybit exchange via ccxt.
Notion has been removed. A self-hosted Knowledge Hub (PostgreSQL + pgvector) replaces it.

## Mandatory Document References
Before ANY code change, check these docs:
- `docs/13_security_design.md` — Security rules (ABSOLUTE, never override)
- `docs/07_aave_operation_logic.md` — Aave risk management
- `docs/08_automation_rules.md` — Trading limits and cooldowns
- `docs/14_test_strategy.md` — Testing requirements
- `docs/05_ai_judgement_rules.md` — BUY/SELL/HOLD logic

## Absolute Security Rules
1. NO secrets in code — all via environment variables
2. NO .env files in Git — .gitignore enforced
3. NO tokens/keys in logs — mask to first 6 + last 4 chars
4. Aave operations MUST check: Health Factor, cooldown (10min), daily limit (30%)
5. Emergency stop flag uses OR logic — manual stop can NEVER be overwritten by automation
6. Max single trade: 10% of total assets
7. LLM output MUST be JSON Schema validated — parse failure → HOLD

## Architecture Decisions
- Backend: FastAPI (Python 3.11), Hetzner VPS, Docker Compose
- Frontend: Next.js App Router, Cloudflare Pages
- DB: PostgreSQL + pgvector (HNSW index, NOT IVFFlat)
- Exchange: Bybit primary (ccxt), OKX backup
- AI Judge: Claude Opus 4.6 primary → GPT-4o cross-verify (BUY/SELL only)
- Rule engine executes BEFORE LLM calls (saves cost, enforces safety)

## Coding Standards
- Python: ruff + mypy strict mode
- TypeScript: strict + ESLint
- Financial calculations: Decimal type ONLY (never float)
- All new features require tests (coverage 80%+)
- Imports: absolute paths from `app.*`

## Multi-LLM Development Roles
This project uses multiple LLMs. Cursor models should follow these patterns:

### Tab / Inline Edit (Sonnet 4.5 — fast implementation)
- CRUD, boilerplate, test code, docs
- Single-file changes, refactoring
- Frontend components

### Composer Agent (Opus 4.6 — architecture)
- New module design, multi-file changes
- Aave / security-critical code (ALWAYS Opus)
- Cross-module integration
- When prompt includes "@codebase" scope

### Automated Review (Codex 5.3 — GitHub Actions)
- Every PR is auto-reviewed by `.github/workflows/codex-review.yml`
- Checks: security, type errors, dead code, test coverage
- Critical issues block merge

### Escalation Rule
If Sonnet cannot resolve a bug in 2 attempts → switch to Opus in Composer.
If the change touches Aave, exchange, or auth code → use Opus from the start.

## Module Structure
```
backend/app/
├── knowledge/  # NEW: replaces notion/ — PostgreSQL + pgvector
├── exchange/   # NEW: ccxt abstraction (Bybit/OKX)
├── ai/         # ENHANCED: multi-LLM judge with JSON Schema
├── aave/       # UPGRADE: DummyClient → web3.py
├── bots/       # KEEP: OctoBot signal integration
├── automation/ # KEEP: monitoring, reporting, emergency stop
└── notifications/ # KEEP: Slack/LINE
```

## API Naming Convention
- Old: POST /notion/ingest → New: POST /knowledge/items
- Old: Notion write-back → New: PUT /knowledge/items/{id}/status
- New: POST /knowledge/search (RAG vector search)
- New: POST /exchange/order (ccxt → Bybit)

## When Writing Aave Code
- ALWAYS check Health Factor before any operation
- ALWAYS enforce 10-minute cooldown
- ALWAYS use Decimal, never float
- ALWAYS handle AaveClientError gracefully (return NOOP, not crash)
- ALWAYS log operations with masked addresses

## When Writing AI/LLM Code
- ALWAYS use structured JSON output
- ALWAYS validate with Pydantic before acting on LLM response
- ALWAYS fall back to HOLD on any error/timeout/parse failure
- NEVER log full prompts (may contain sensitive market data)
- NEVER call LLM if rule engine already determined HOLD

## When Writing Exchange Code
- ALWAYS use ccxt async (ccxt.async_support)
- ALWAYS use sandbox mode for PoC/MVP (sandbox=True)
- ALWAYS handle rate limits with exponential backoff
- NEVER hardcode exchange-specific logic — abstract via ccxt

## When Writing Tests
- Unit tests: test one function, mock external calls
- VCR tests for LLM: record real response once, replay in CI
- Aave tests: use Sepolia testnet stubs
- Exchange tests: use Bybit Sandbox or ccxt mock
- Coverage: 80% minimum, enforced in CI

## Branch Rules
- feature/* → dev → staging → main
- No direct push to main
- All PRs require: CI pass + Codex review + human approval
- Aave/security PRs require Opus review before merge